  "full_text": "Hey. I just woke up. What? Fucking Davis. And I'm fucking awake. I got my fucking beers. So, like, can you imagine being homeless? Pretty righteous. Nothing like a beer. It's a warm beer. It doesn't really feel very good. What you have to do is you have to walk, like, you have to walk, like, two miles, then get your beer and bring it back home. That's how you earn your beer. i have legs of steel you saw you saw them you saw my legs of steel i got like the you got wimpy arms legs of steel they're not legs of steel they're they're beautiful i have beautiful legs let me see my beautiful legs just saying so am i evil i'm a man so what do we do when we're my job is to entertain God. I wonder what's going to happen. Am I evil? God says, you're my man. Great-hearted souls. Blessed are the peacemakers. They shall be called sons of God. Peacemakers release tension. They don't intensify it. Peacemakers seek solutions and find no delight in arguments. Peacemakers calm the waters. They don't trouble them. Peacemakers work hard to keep an offense from occurring. If it has occurred, they strive for resolution. Peacemakers lower their voice rather than raise them. Peacemakers generate more light than heat. Blessed are such great-hearted souls. Make no mistake, however. Peacemaker is not synonymous with appeaser. It is not peace at any price. There are limits. Smiling at wrongdoing or erroneous teaching doesn't simplify life. It complicates it. When Christ blessed the peacemakers, he was extolling the value of doing all we can to maintain harmony and support unity. His interest was making peace where peace is an appropriate objective. You simply don't know the joy of being homeless. And drunk. And happy. And homeless. Am I evil?",
